/* V89 — THE BLOOM's cast. Six things that grew, wearing the six roles the game already speaks.

   ---- What this is, and what it deliberately is not ----

   V87 made a map "a cast plus a list of stages" and left index 1 of `V87_ROSTERS` empty on purpose,
   so map two shipped playable with map one's machines while its own cast was drawn. This is that
   index. It adds no mechanic, no field on `o`, and no branch anywhere else: six draw functions and
   six palettes, installed through the table V87 already rewrites.

   The roles are untouched and that is the whole point. `normal / armored / bomb / shard / splitter /
   rival` are what V63 sizes a blast from, what V64 spaces a formation against, what V65 seals, what
   V72 splits, what V76 gathers and what V83 refunds on. A player crossing into THE BLOOM meets a
   cast they have never seen and a game they already know how to play.

   ---- The silhouette contract ----

   `03j.part` decided each archetype answers one question differently, and that decision is the
   reason a crowd is readable at five taps a second on a phone. It is inherited here as a contract
   rather than as a style, because the player learned it in map one and has to be able to spend it
   in map two BEFORE they know what any of these things are:

     normal    the thinnest thing        dart          ->  seed drifter, a husk with two long blades
     armored   the widest thing          walker        ->  bracket husk, shelves out past its feet
     bomb      the only true circle      sphere        ->  seed gall, a swollen pod about to split
     shard     the tall spike            crystal       ->  bramble thorn, barbed, no head
     splitter  the twin-headed one       two heads     ->  twin bud on a root cluster
     rival     the only humanoid         humanoid      ->  the grown one, shoulders, waist, cloak

   Each is drawn a different way inside the same outline family. The machines are hard planes with
   fasteners; these are blades, husks, roots and buds, and every mass that had a bolted seam has a
   growth ring instead. Nothing here is cute and nothing here is a mushroom joke: it is overgrowth
   that has learned to defend itself, which mostly means thorns pointing outward and a lot of dark
   fibrous bulk under a thin bright skin.

   ---- Everything goes through v15Carapace, because V81 lives there ----

   V81 wraps that one helper to put a heavy near-black contour behind every body and a bright rim
   along its upper-left edge, and it removes body-sized halos. That treatment is what makes a
   silhouette survive a lit planet passing behind it, so every primary mass here is a `v15Carapace`
   call and nothing is a free-floating fill.

   Anything that is NOT a carapace -- blades, shelves, barbs, petals, crowns -- gets the same
   treatment by hand through `v89Plate`, which strokes the contour first and fills over its inner
   half. Drawn the other way round the outline straddles the path and eats a third of a thin blade's
   own colour, which is exactly the failure V81's header describes.

   ---- Colour, chosen against V81's convergence rather than in spite of it ----

   V81 pulls every hostile's hue half-way toward the world's own enemy colour and takes 30% of its
   saturation, leaving lightness alone. That has a consequence worth stating, because it caught the
   first draft of this palette: two hues 30 degrees apart end up 15 apart, and in a violet world a
   rot-red and a fungal gold both arrive as the same dull magenta. Neighbouring hues are not a
   palette here, they are a collision waiting for world four.

   So the cast is spread across a wide arc and, more importantly, laddered by LIGHTNESS, which
   convergence does not touch:

     shard    ~.78  bleached bone      the brightest thing on the field, and the thinnest tall one
     normal   ~.61  new growth
     splitter ~.54  fungal gold
     bomb     ~.42  rot red
     armored  ~.31  wet bark           the darkest mass, and the biggest
     rival     n/a  bruise violet      wears the player's skin in play; this is its preview colour

   Eyes and glows are the one channel V81 leaves alone, so they are deliberately NOT used to tell
   the archetypes apart -- they are all sporelight, one narrow band of acid yellow-green, because a
   single shared eye colour is what says these six are one organism rather than six species. The
   gall is the exception and gets amber: on that one creature the eye is a fuse readout.

   ---- Rules honoured ----

   No `lighter` on a body, no gradient of my own filling a body, no `shadowBlur`. The only additive
   marks are `v15Eye` and one `v15Spark` on the gall's bud, which is what those helpers exist for.
   `rng()` is never called -- it is the seeded stream the spawner draws from and a cosmetic read
   would desynchronise a replayed seed. Anything that must not flicker frame to frame is derived
   from `o.anim`, which is per creature and fixed for its life.

   ---- Things that did not work, kept here so they are not retried ----

   - Translucent petals layered over the body. Without additive blending they turn to mud against a
     dark arena, and with it they are a body glow by another name. Petals here are opaque plates.
   - Orbiting spore motes around the thorn, mirroring the crystal wraith's shards. At speed they
     read as a halo, which is the thing V81 removed; the barbs do the same job and stay attached.
   - A rot-red gall next to a gold splitter, both at mid lightness. Distinct at desk size, identical
     after convergence in world one. The gall was darkened until the pair separated in monochrome.
   - Asymmetric outlines for an "grown wrong" feel. Mirrored shapes read as creatures and asymmetric
     ones read as damage; the wrongness is carried by the barbs and the split seams instead. */

const V89={
 /* The hand-rolled equivalent of V81.edge, for the plates that do not pass through v15Carapace.
    Slightly lighter than V81's 3.6 because these shapes are smaller and a 3.6 contour on a 6-unit
    barb is a black spike with a hint of colour in it. */
 edge:3,
 ink:'rgba(3,8,5,.9)'
};

/* A flat organic plate with its own contour: stroke first, fill over the inner half. */
function v89Plate(c,points,fill,alpha=1){
 c.save();
 c.globalAlpha=alpha;
 c.lineJoin='round';
 c.strokeStyle=V89.ink;
 c.lineWidth=V89.edge;
 v14Art.poly(c,points);
 c.stroke();
 c.fillStyle=fill;
 v14Art.poly(c,points);
 c.fill();
 c.restore();
}

/* A dark wash over part of a plate. Flat plates read as cut card next to a carapace, which has
   V19's ramp and seams inside it; one shaded region is enough to give a blade a fold and costs a
   fill. Deliberately near-black rather than a darker tint of the body: a tint reads as a second
   material, shadow reads as the same material bending. */
function v89Shade(c,points,alpha=.34){
 c.save();
 c.globalAlpha=alpha;
 c.fillStyle='#050a04';
 v14Art.poly(c,points);
 c.fill();
 c.restore();
}

/* Fibre running through a mass, clipped to it. The organic answer to V19's panel seams, which are
   still drawn underneath by the shared carapace and read as growth rings rather than plating. */
function v89Grain(c,points,color,lines,alpha=.4){
 c.save();
 v14Art.poly(c,points);
 c.clip();
 c.globalAlpha=alpha;
 c.strokeStyle=color;
 c.lineWidth=1.1;
 c.lineCap='round';
 for(const [ax,ay,bx,by] of lines){c.beginPath();c.moveTo(ax,ay);c.lineTo(bx,by);c.stroke();}
 c.restore();
}

/* Spore freckles. A fixed table rather than a random one: `rng()` belongs to the spawner and a
   per-frame `Math.random()` here would make the creature crawl. */
function v89Dots(c,list,color,alpha=.55){
 c.save();
 c.globalAlpha=alpha;
 c.fillStyle=color;
 for(const [x,y,r] of list){c.beginPath();c.arc(x,y,r,0,Math.PI*2);c.fill();}
 c.restore();
}

/* The one shape in the cast that has to be a circle, built once. Twelve sides with a ±1.2 unit
   wobble off the radius so it is a grown thing rather than a ball bearing -- small enough that it
   is still unmistakably the only round creature on the field, which is the contract. */
const V89_GALL=(()=>{
 const points=[];
 for(let i=0;i<12;i++){
  const a=i/12*Math.PI*2-Math.PI/2;
  const r=26+((i*7)%3-1)*1.2;
  points.push([Math.cos(a)*r,Math.sin(a)*r+1]);
 }
 return points;
})();

const V89_CREATURES={
 /* SEED DRIFTER — the thinnest thing. A husk barely wider than its own eye, two long blades swept
    back and down, and three root threads trailing under it. Vertical from crest to thread is more
    than three times its width, which is the read: everything else in this cast is bulk. */
 normal(c,o,pal,t){
  const anim=o.anim||0;
  const drift=Math.sin(t*3.2+anim)*.15;
  const sway=Math.sin(t*2.4+anim)*4;

  /* Blades sweep DOWN rather than out. Out is a wing and a wing is width; down keeps the whole
     creature inside a narrow vertical envelope while still giving it something to fall on. */
  for(const side of [-1,1]){
   c.save();
   c.translate(side*4,-12);
   c.rotate(side*(drift+.1));
   /* Serrated along the trailing edge only. A leaf notched all the way round reads as damage; one
      toothed edge reads as a leaf. */
   const blade=[[0,-5],[side*11,-4],[side*28,16],[side*20,18],[side*22,24],[side*13,22],[side*13,27],[side*3,9]];
   v89Plate(c,blade,pal.body,.95);
   v89Shade(c,[[side*3,9],[side*13,27],[side*22,24],[side*18,15]],.45);
   c.save();
   c.globalAlpha=.5;
   c.strokeStyle=pal.trim;
   c.lineWidth=1.2;
   c.lineCap='round';
   c.beginPath();c.moveTo(side*2,-2);c.lineTo(side*24,16);c.stroke();
   c.beginPath();c.moveTo(side*8,1);c.lineTo(side*13,11);c.stroke();
   c.beginPath();c.moveTo(side*14,7);c.lineTo(side*18,17);c.stroke();
   c.restore();
   c.restore();
  }

  /* Threads. One long centre thread with two short ones beside it, rather than three of a length:
     three equal threads read as legs and legs belong to the husk and the twin bud. */
  v14Art.limb(c,-4,20,-8+sway*.3,36,2.6,pal.body);
  v14Art.limb(c,4,20,8-sway*.3,38,2.6,pal.body);
  v14Art.limb(c,0,22,sway*.5,57,2.2,pal.body);

  /* Crest, behind the body so the carapace covers its root. */
  v89Plate(c,[[0,-49],[4,-32],[-4,-32]],pal.trim,.92);

  v15Carapace(c,pal,[[0,-35],[6,-19],[7,4],[4,24],[-4,24],[-7,4],[-6,-19]],-7,-33,7,22);
  c.fillStyle='#080f06';
  v14Art.poly(c,[[0,-30],[5,-21],[4,-9],[-4,-9],[-5,-21]]);c.fill();
  /* Same dark core as the thorn, and for the same reason: V19's vent and specular are additive and
     clipped to the body, so on a fourteen-unit-wide body they cover most of it and the husk loses
     its value. Knocked back rather than blacked out, because a husk with something lit inside it is
     what this creature is. */
  c.save();
  c.globalAlpha=.32;
  c.fillStyle='#0b1209';
  v14Art.poly(c,[[-4.5,-10],[4.5,-10],[3.2,22],[-3.2,22]]);c.fill();
  c.restore();
  v15Eye(c,0,-18,5,pal);
  v89Dots(c,[[-3,2,1.2],[3,8,1],[-2,14,.9]],pal.trim,.5);
 },

 /* BRACKET HUSK — the widest thing. Shelf growths out past its own feet, a squat woody mass and a
    crown of thorns: wider than it is tall by a clear margin, which no other creature here is. */
 armored(c,o,pal,t){
  const anim=o.anim||0;
  const stomp=Math.sin(t*2.4+anim)*2.4;

  /* Legs clear of the body, which reaches x=±26 and y=27: a leg tucked under a mass that wide is a
     leg nobody sees, and then the husk is a rock rather than a thing that walks toward you. */
  v14Art.limb(c,-15,19,-25,39+stomp,12,pal.body);
  v14Art.limb(c,15,19,25,39-stomp,12,pal.body);
  /* Root toes. A walker with no feet is furniture; a grown thing with no roots is a rock. Drawn as
     plates rather than as flat black fills -- a black shape under a dark body reads as a hole. */
  for(const side of [-1,1]){
   const y=39+stomp*side;
   v89Plate(c,[[side*17,y-6],[side*35,y-3],[side*33,y+6],[side*16,y+7]],pal.body,.9);
   v89Shade(c,[[side*17,y+1],[side*34,y],[side*33,y+6],[side*16,y+7]],.45);
  }

  /* The shelves. These are the silhouette: they take the outline out to ±46 while the body stops at
     30, so the widest point of this creature is a growth rather than its shoulders -- and they have
     to clear the body by enough that V81's contour on each does not merge them into one blob. */
  for(const side of [-1,1]){
   v89Plate(c,[[side*14,-21],[side*36,-19],[side*46,-4],[side*34,9],[side*14,4]],pal.body,1);
   v89Shade(c,[[side*14,-3],[side*41,-3],[side*34,9],[side*14,4]],.4);
   c.save();
   c.globalAlpha=.42;
   c.strokeStyle=pal.trim;
   c.lineWidth=1.2;
   for(let i=1;i<=3;i++){
    c.beginPath();
    c.moveTo(side*(16+i*3),-18+i*4.5);
    c.lineTo(side*(34+i*2),-11+i*4);
    c.stroke();
   }
   c.restore();
  }

  v15Carapace(c,pal,[[-16,-23],[16,-23],[30,-6],[26,12],[13,27],[-13,27],[-26,12],[-30,-6]],-30,-23,30,27);

  /* Thorns along the top edge. Defence, and a hard upper contour so the mass does not read as a
     pebble once the shelves are lost at distance. */
  for(const [x,h] of [[-12,-33],[0,-38],[12,-33]])v89Plate(c,[[x-4.5,-21],[x,h],[x+4.5,-21]],pal.trim,.9);

  c.fillStyle='#070d05';
  v14Art.poly(c,[[-21,-21],[21,-21],[17,-5],[-17,-5]]);c.fill();
  /* Three eyes in a row rather than one bar: the same wide horizontal face the sentinel had, said
     in an animal's grammar instead of a machine's. */
  v15Eye(c,-10,-13,3.4,pal);
  v15Eye(c,0,-13.5,4.4,pal);
  v15Eye(c,10,-13,3.4,pal);
  v89Dots(c,[[-19,6,1.6],[-11,15,1.3],[6,9,1.5],[17,4,1.2],[0,20,1.4]],pal.trim,.45);
 },

 /* SEED GALL — the only true circle. A swollen pod with the split already started and something
    lit inside it, four short roots clamped to the shell, and a stalk carrying a bud that is the
    fuse. Round is the shape everybody already reads as "this is going to go off". */
 bomb(c,o,pal,t){
  const anim=o.anim||0;
  const beat=.5+.5*Math.sin(t*9+anim);

  for(const side of [-1,1]){
   v14Art.limb(c,side*19,6,side*28,17,5,pal.body);
   v14Art.limb(c,side*11,24,side*15,35,5,pal.body);
  }

  v15Carapace(c,pal,V89_GALL,-24,-24,24,24);

  /* The split. Flat bright strokes rather than additive ones -- the shell is a body and V81's rule
     is that bodies do not emit. The pulse is carried by alpha and line width, which reads as a
     pressure change rather than as a lamp. */
  c.save();
  v14Art.poly(c,V89_GALL);
  c.clip();
  /* Dark first, hot line beside it. A bright line alone is a scratch on the surface; a dark
     fissure with light coming up one side of it is a shell that has come apart. */
  c.lineJoin='round';
  c.lineCap='round';
  /* One split that runs the whole shell top to bottom with two short branches off it, rather than
     three unrelated cracks. Three read as a graphic applied to the surface; one read as a seam the
     thing is failing along, which is what it is. */
  const fissure=[
   [[1,-27],[5,-14],[-3,-2],[3,11],[-4,26]],
   [[-3,-2],[-16,-7],[-21,-13]],
   [[3,11],[15,9],[21,15]]
  ];
  for(const line of fissure){
   c.globalAlpha=.8;
   c.strokeStyle='#100704';
   c.lineWidth=3.4;
   c.beginPath();c.moveTo(line[0][0],line[0][1]);
   for(let i=1;i<line.length;i++)c.lineTo(line[i][0],line[i][1]);
   c.stroke();
   c.globalAlpha=.45+beat*.45;
   c.strokeStyle=pal.eye;
   c.lineWidth=1.2+beat*.7;
   c.translate(-.9,-.9);
   c.beginPath();c.moveTo(line[0][0],line[0][1]);
   for(let i=1;i<line.length;i++)c.lineTo(line[i][0],line[i][1]);
   c.stroke();
   c.translate(.9,.9);
  }
  c.restore();

  /* Ocelli in a shallow arc rather than two big eyes in a rounded hollow. The hollow-plus-pair is a
     face, and a face on a round body is a cartoon -- which the raider gets away with because it is
     unmistakably a machine and this thing is not. Three small ones set into a slot read as an
     animal that has eyes, which is a different and much less friendly statement. */
  c.fillStyle='#140806';
  v14Art.poly(c,[[-16,-21],[16,-21],[12,-6],[-12,-6]]);c.fill();
  v15Eye(c,0,-17,3.6,pal,1+beat);
  v15Eye(c,-8,-10,3,pal,1+beat*.6);
  v15Eye(c,8,-10,3,pal,1+beat*.6);

  /* Stalk and bud. The one additive mark on this creature, and it is a fuse readout. */
  c.save();
  c.strokeStyle=pal.trim;
  c.lineWidth=2.6;
  c.lineCap='round';
  c.beginPath();c.moveTo(3,-26);c.quadraticCurveTo(13,-39,21,-34);c.stroke();
  c.restore();
  v89Plate(c,[[21,-40],[26,-34],[21,-28],[16,-34]],pal.trim,.9);
  v15Spark(c,21,-34,'#ffcf6a',12,.7+beat*.6);

  v89Dots(c,[[-16,10,1.5],[-6,18,1.2],[12,10,1.4],[16,-2,1.1]],pal.trim,.4);
 },

 /* BRAMBLE THORN — the tall spike. No head, no limbs to speak of, barbs hooking up and out, and a
    single eye low on the shaft. Taller than anything else in the cast and the palest, so it is the
    one that still reads when everything else has gone to shape. */
 shard(c,o,pal,t){
  const anim=o.anim||0;
  const lean=Math.sin(t*1.7+anim)*.045;

  c.save();
  c.rotate(lean);

  /* Barbs first so the shaft covers their roots. Hooked, and all pointing up-and-out: a thorn is
     read by which way it would go into you. */
  /* Rooted on the shaft, not floating beside it: every barb's first and last point sit inside the
     outline, so the plate is cut off by the body rather than meeting it at a seam. Uneven lengths,
     because a thorn that alternates evenly is a fish bone. */
  for(const barb of [
   [[-7,-8],[-30,-24],[-21,-9],[-8,3]],
   [[7,0],[32,-13],[22,2],[8,12]],
   [[-6,11],[-24,3],[-16,13],[-5,19]],
   [[5,-20],[20,-38],[15,-23],[6,-13]],
   [[6,17],[19,15],[13,22],[5,24]]
  ]){
   v89Plate(c,barb,pal.body,1);
   v89Shade(c,[barb[0],barb[2],barb[3]],.42);
  }

  /* Root stubs. Three, short, so the base is anchored without becoming legs -- legs would make it
     a walker and the walker is somebody else. */
  v14Art.limb(c,-4,26,-11,38,3.4,pal.body);
  v14Art.limb(c,4,26,11,38,3.4,pal.body);
  v14Art.limb(c,0,28,0,42,3,pal.body);

  /* The tip hooks. A straight symmetrical spike is a mineral -- it is what the crystal wraith is,
     and drawing it again in green would be the same creature in a costume. A shaft that leans off
     its own axis at the top is something that grew toward something. */
  const shaft=[[7,-48],[11,-26],[9,-8],[5,12],[0,33],[-7,10],[-9,-16],[-4,-34]];
  v15Carapace(c,pal,shaft,-9,-46,11,30);
  /* Fibre running the length of the shaft, not facets: this grew, it was not cut. */
  v89Grain(c,shaft,'#0a1008',[[4,-44,-4,30],[8,-40,2,28],[-2,-38,-7,20],[0,-46,3,32]],.5);
  /* A dark core under V19's additive vent and specular, which between them lift a narrow body's
     whole interior. Painted after the carapace so it survives them, and inset so the rim V81 put
     along the lit edge is untouched -- that rim is the whole silhouette on a shape this thin.

     Not, however, the fix for the soft bloom that photographs over the middle of the drifter and
     the thorn in the zoo harness. That was chased and attributed: it survives `v19PanelSeams`,
     `v15Eye` and `drawOrb` all stubbed out, and map one's scout and wraith carry the identical mark
     in the identical place. It is not coming from the creature art and it is not worth drawing
     around. */
  c.save();
  c.globalAlpha=.5;
  c.fillStyle='#0b1209';
  v14Art.poly(c,[[5,-40],[8,-24],[6,-8],[3,10],[0,26],[-5,9],[-6,-15],[-2,-31]]);c.fill();
  c.restore();

  /* Small, low, and dimmed. The thorn has no head, so the eye is the only thing on it that reads as
     alive -- but a large one halfway up the shaft turns the creature into a lamp on a stick, which
     is the crystal wraith's failure and the wraith at least had a radial heart gradient to blame. */
  v15Eye(c,1,8,3.6,pal,.8);
  v89Dots(c,[[-3,-26,1.1],[3,-14,1],[-2,22,1.1]],pal.trim,.45);
  c.restore();
 },

 /* TWIN BUD — the two-headed one, bottom-heavy on a cluster of roots, with the seam it will come
    apart along already open down the middle. Two buds craning off one base is the same statement
    the twin beast made, and it is the only creature here with a horizontal centre of mass. */
 splitter(c,o,pal,t){
  const anim=o.anim||0;
  const sway=Math.sin(t*3+anim)*3;

  v14Art.limb(c,-17,14,-31,30+sway,5,pal.body);
  v14Art.limb(c,17,14,31,30-sway,5,pal.body);
  v14Art.limb(c,-11,24,-17,41,6,pal.body);
  v14Art.limb(c,11,24,17,41,6,pal.body);
  v14Art.limb(c,-4,26,-5,44,5,pal.body);
  v14Art.limb(c,4,26,5,44,5,pal.body);

  const base=[[-16,-8],[16,-8],[28,8],[18,30],[-18,30],[-28,8]];
  v15Carapace(c,pal,base,-28,-9,28,29);
  /* Vertical fibre, to cut across V19's horizontal seams. Left alone the base is the one mass in
     this cast that still reads as a panelled hull. */
  v89Grain(c,base,'#080d05',[[-19,-10,-14,32],[-8,-10,-6,32],[8,-10,7,32],[19,-10,15,32]],.4);

  /* The seam. Dark and dashed, with a thin sap line beside it -- the cue that this one is already
     failing along a line the player gets to choose the position of. */
  c.save();
  v14Art.poly(c,base);
  c.clip();
  c.globalAlpha=.65;
  c.strokeStyle='#070d05';
  c.lineWidth=2.6;
  c.setLineDash([4,4]);
  c.beginPath();c.moveTo(0,-10);c.lineTo(0,31);c.stroke();
  c.globalAlpha=.35;
  c.strokeStyle=pal.trim;
  c.lineWidth=1;
  c.setLineDash([]);
  c.beginPath();c.moveTo(2,-10);c.lineTo(2,31);c.stroke();
  c.restore();

  for(const side of [-1,1]){
   c.save();
   v14Art.limb(c,side*6,-6,side*11,-17,5,pal.body);
   c.translate(side*11,-21+sway*side*.2);
   c.rotate(side*.26);
   /* Sepals behind the bud, so each head has a spiked collar and the pair cannot merge into one
      blob when they overlap at distance. */
   for(const s of [-1,1])v89Plate(c,[[s*3,-6],[s*15,-19],[s*7,-2]],pal.body,.95);
   /* A bud rather than a head: pointed at the top, widest below the middle, and with one petal
      peeled back off the outer side. Boxy heads on stalks read as sensor pods, which is the map
      the player just left. */
   v89Plate(c,[[side*2,-16],[side*13,-9],[side*11,4],[side*1,2]],pal.body,.9);
   v15Carapace(c,pal,[[0,-20],[7,-9],[9,3],[4,9],[-4,9],[-9,3],[-7,-9]],-9,-20,9,9);
   c.fillStyle='#0a0e05';
   v14Art.poly(c,[[-6,-12],[6,-12],[5,1],[-5,1]]);c.fill();
   v15Eye(c,0,-6,4,pal);
   c.restore();
  }

  v89Dots(c,[[-20,12,1.5],[-9,22,1.2],[13,16,1.4],[21,6,1.2]],pal.trim,.45);
 },

 /* THE GROWN ONE — the only humanoid. Shoulders, a waist, a flared hip and a cloak of hanging
    fronds: a person-shaped thing where everything else is a thing-shaped thing. It wears the
    player's own skin colours in play, so its silhouette is carrying the whole of its identity. */
 rival(c,o,pal,t){
  const anim=o.anim||0;
  const cape=Math.sin(t*2.2+anim)*5;

  /* Cloak: leaf-edged rather than cut cloth, ragged along the bottom. */
  for(const side of [-1,1]){
   v89Plate(c,[
    [side*13,-15],[side*34,-25-cape*side],[side*31,2],[side*23,7],
    [side*26,17],[side*16,15],[side*18,27],[side*10,22],[side*11,32]
   ],pal.body,.72);
  }

  /* Two-segment arms hanging at the sides. One straight bar per side reads as a strut; an elbow
     reads as a limb, and the limb is half of what makes this thing a person. */
  for(const side of [-1,1]){
   v14Art.limb(c,side*18,-13,side*27,3,7,pal.body);
   v14Art.limb(c,side*27,3,side*23,21,6,pal.body);
   v89Plate(c,[[side*20,20],[side*27,20],[side*26,28],[side*20,27]],pal.body,.95);
  }

  const body=[[0,-25],[22,-15],[12,4],[15,20],[8,33],[-8,33],[-15,20],[-12,4],[-22,-15]];
  v15Carapace(c,pal,body,-22,-25,22,33);
  v89Grain(c,body,'#0a0e05',[[-10,-16,-4,30],[10,-16,4,30],[0,-20,0,30]],.35);

  /* A head on a neck, clear of the shoulders. Map one's warlord puts its visor into the top of its
     own torso, and it survives that because it is plated and symmetrical and reads as a suit. This
     one is a grown thing and needs the join stated: without a neck it was a crest on a shield. */
  v14Art.limb(c,0,-22,0,-30,7,pal.body);
  v15Carapace(c,pal,[[0,-46],[10,-39],[11,-28],[0,-22],[-11,-28],[-10,-39]],-11,-46,11,-22);

  /* A seed set in the chest. The one place on this creature where something is visibly alive. */
  c.save();
  c.globalAlpha=.9;
  c.fillStyle=pal.trim;
  v14Art.poly(c,[[0,-14],[7,-1],[0,12],[-7,-1]]);c.fill();
  c.restore();
  c.save();
  c.globalAlpha=.6;
  c.fillStyle='#0a0e05';
  v14Art.poly(c,[[0,-8],[3.5,-1],[0,6],[-3.5,-1]]);c.fill();
  c.restore();

  c.fillStyle='#070c06';
  v14Art.poly(c,[[-9,-42],[9,-42],[8,-28],[-8,-28]]);c.fill();
  c.save();
  c.translate(0,-35);
  v15Halo(c,pal.glow,17);
  c.fillStyle=pal.glow;
  v14Art.round(c,-7,-2.5,14,5,2.5);c.fill();
  c.restore();

  /* Crown: branched thorns, two a side, uneven lengths. A single pair reads as horns and horns are
     the twin bud's; a branched crown reads as a head that kept growing. */
  for(const side of [-1,1]){
   v89Plate(c,[[side*6,-44],[side*13,-62],[side*11,-42]],pal.trim,.92);
   v89Plate(c,[[side*10,-40],[side*23,-52],[side*14,-36]],pal.trim,.85);
  }
 }
};

/* ---- The palette --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Laddered by lightness for the reason in the header: convergence takes half the hue and none of
   the value, so value is the channel that is still there in world four. Trims are the rim V81
   strokes along the lit edge, so each is a clear step above its body rather than a tint of it.

   `glow` is darker than it looks like it should be, and that is measured rather than taste. V19
   paints an additive vent band across the middle of every carapace in `pal.glow`, so a pale glow is
   a white bar through the centre of the creature -- on the drifter and the thorn, both of which are
   narrow, the band is as wide as the body and the middle of the silhouette disappears into it.
   Mid-value saturated glows keep the vent as an interior light and keep the hue, which matters
   because `glow` and `eye` are the two channels V81's convergence leaves alone. */

const V89_PALETTE={
 normal:{body:'#a6d861',trim:'#e6f7b4',glow:'#79b234',eye:'#eaffb0'},
 armored:{body:'#6d5a33',trim:'#c9ae70',glow:'#87693a',eye:'#ffe08e'},
 bomb:{body:'#b03a28',trim:'#ffb590',glow:'#d8532a',eye:'#ffce6a'},
 shard:{body:'#d8dfb0',trim:'#f8ffe0',glow:'#93aa62',eye:'#f4ffd2'},
 splitter:{body:'#d9a13a',trim:'#ffe6a6',glow:'#c08e2c',eye:'#fff2bc'},
 /* The warlord's replacement takes the player's skin from `v15Skin` in play, so these colours are
    what the armory preview and any UI that draws a rival outside a run will use. */
 rival:{body:'#8e5ab4',trim:'#ead0f8',glow:'#b87fe2',eye:'#ffffff'}
};

V87_ROSTERS.push({
 id:'bloom',
 name:'THE BLOOM',
 creatures:Object.assign({},V89_CREATURES),
 palette:JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(V89_PALETTE))
});

/* V87 installs the roster on load, and on load this file had not run yet -- so a player standing in
   map two right now is holding map one's cast until the next reload. Reinstalling the map they are
   already in swaps the tables under them with no announcement and no progress movement, which is
   the cheapest correct answer: `v87Install` banks and restores the same numbers either way. */
v87Install(v87State.map,false);
